  <h2>A stable base lets you lift more</h2>
  <p>Three lab studies tested the bench press on stable and unstable surfaces. They did not all agree, and they used different loads.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Saeterbakken and Fimland (2013)</strong> had 16 resistance-trained men do 6 rep max bench presses on a normal bench, a balance cushion and a Swiss ball. Relative to the stable bench, the 6RM load was about 93 percent on the cushion and about 92 percent on the ball. Chest muscle activity was about 90 percent (cushion) and 81 percent (ball), and triceps activity about 79 and 69 percent. The authors concluded that unstable surfaces had an inferior effect on prime mover activation and strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Goodman and colleagues (2008)</strong> tested 13 people for a 1RM chest press on a flat bench and an exercise ball. They found no difference in 1RM or muscle activity between the two.</li>
    <li><strong>Norwood and colleagues (2007)</strong> measured trunk and calf muscle activity during a 9.1 kg bench press and found it rose as the setup became less stable. That is a very light load, so it says little about heavy benching.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Put together, an unstable base does not make the prime movers work harder in the 6RM study, and one study found it did not change the 1RM. None of these studies followed lifters over time, so none tested whether training unstable builds a bigger bench.</p>

  <h2>Is a bigger arch stronger?</h2>
  <p>García-Ramos and colleagues (2021) tested 11 competitive male powerlifters on two bench setups: a flat one with a natural lumbar arch and moderate scapular retraction, and an arched one with a pronounced lumbar arch and retraction. The 1RM was 115.9 kg flat and 115.7 kg arched, not a significant difference. Bar speed at the same loads did not differ either.</p>
  <p>The authors concluded that competitive powerlifters do not necessarily lift more with the arched setup. This is one small study of men who already compete, so it does not show what is best for you. It does suggest that copying a dramatic arch is not a shortcut.</p>

  <h2>What the research does not tell us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We did not find a trial on foot position, such as feet flat or tucked under the bench.</li>
    <li>We did not find studies on cues for tightness, lift-off, breathing or knuckle alignment and their effect on bench strength.</li>
    <li>We did not find trials testing whether strengthening the rotator cuff, serratus, lats or core makes a shaky bench steadier.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So this page does not tell you to strengthen a specific muscle or hold a specific position. Nothing we found supports it.</p>

  <h2>Practical points</h2>
  <p>These are general coaching points, not findings from the studies above.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Film a heavy set from the side and from the front. Whether the bar drifts sideways, forward or back, or one side rises first, tells you more than a guess.</li>
    <li>If it only wobbles on the heaviest reps, the weight may be ahead of your technique. Drop the load a little and repeat. Jordan says good form is the foundation of everything and that you should not progress too fast.</li>
    <li>If one side is consistently different, see our page on <a href="/bench-press-left-right-imbalance/">left-right imbalance</a>.</li>
    <li>For a bar that travels the wrong way, see <a href="/bench-press-technique/bar-path/">bar path</a>, and for lower-body setup see <a href="/bench-press-technique/leg-drive/">leg drive</a>.</li>
    <li>Use a spotter or safety arms on heavy sets.</li>
  </ul>
